escribe a technical capability: instrumented, image-guided biopsy execution. [Data: Relationships (6955, 7008); Entities (1801, 3293)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Core biopsy/core needle biopsy are defined as tissue-sampling techniques with specific target contexts (e.g., lymph nodes) and procedural settings (e.g., office)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Core needle biopsy is defined as a method using a hollow needle to remove cylindrical tissue cores for examination, and it is described as being used in cancer evaluation, including sampling suspected spread sites such as lymph nodes. This positions core needle biopsy as a staging/diagnostic adjunct when metastatic involvement is a concern. [Data: Entities (838); Relationships (2029)]\\n\\nThe community further notes that core needle biopsy is commonly performed under light sedation by an interventional radiologist, and that it can also be performed by a surgeon in an office setting. This introduces variability in clinical setting and operator, which can affect operational planning (where procedures occur) even when the technique remains consistent. [Data: Entities (838, 5239); Relationships (11610)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Needle instrumentation is differentiated by role: hollow needle as the core device; wide needle as a specific tissue-sample extractor\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The hollow needle is described as a biopsy needle used to obtain tissue (and marrow) specimens, including removing core samples in prostate biopsy and other lesions/tumors. This makes it the core instrument spanning multiple diagnostic contexts in the community’s procedure set. [Data: Entities (1801)]\\n\\nA separate relationship ties “core biopsy” to the hollow needle, explicitly encoding the dependency that the technique uses this instrument. In parallel, a wide needle is described as the needle used in a core needle biopsy to extract a tissue sample (notably described in the context of lymph node sampling), and